Rivet creates projects and runs their native tools from Neovim. Install the plugin, then choose a tutorial for your language. The terminal CLI is optional.

## Install in Neovim

Use Neovim 0.11+ on macOS or Linux, with Git available to your plugin manager. Choose one of the installation methods below.

With Neovim 0.12+'s built-in package manager, add this to your configuration:

```lua
vim.pack.add({
  {
    src = 'https://github.com/guzman109/Rivet',
    name = 'rivet.nvim',
    version = vim.version.range('0.1.x'),
  },
})
require('rivet').setup()
```

With lazy.nvim on Neovim 0.11+, use this plugin specification:

```lua
{
  'guzman109/Rivet',
  name = 'rivet.nvim',
  main = 'rivet',
  version = '0.1.x',
  opts = {},
}
```

Restart Neovim after adding the plugin to your configuration. The version range follows patch releases in the 0.1 series. Both managers store the installed revision in a lockfile.

For other version choices, see [Neovim package specifications](https://neovim.io/doc/user/pack/#vim.pack.Spec) or [lazy.nvim version selection](https://lazy.folke.io/spec/versioning).

Run `:RivetDoctor` to confirm that the command is available. If Neovim does not recognize it, check that the plugin and setup call have loaded. The report checks available tools; a successful build and test verifies the project workflow.

## Choose your first project

| Language | Tools for the first run                                                                                              | Follow next                                              |
| -------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| C++      | CMake 3.30+, Ninja 1.11+, compiler supporting your chosen standard; modules also need compatible dependency scanning | [C++ first project](/docs/first-project/)                |
| C        | CMake 3.30+, Ninja, C17 compiler                                                                                     | [Your first C project](/docs/first-c-project/)           |
| Python   | Python 3.11+                                                                                                         | [Your first Python project](/docs/first-python-project/) |
| Rust     | Cargo and Rust 1.85+ for edition 2024                                                                                | [Your first Rust project](/docs/first-rust-project/)     |
| Lua      | Lua 5.1+ for the application tutorial                                                                                | [Your first Lua project](/docs/first-lua-project/)       |
| Swift    | Swift 6.0+ with SwiftPM                                                                                              | [Your first Swift project](/docs/first-swift-project/)   |

The C++ tutorial uses C++26 and named modules. For a C++20 build using conventional headers, use the [CLI example](/docs/getting-started/#create-a-first-project).

Each tutorial creates an application, adds code, and tests the result. See [Compatibility](/docs/compatibility/) for tested toolchains and [Doctor coverage](/docs/doctor/) for what the health check verifies.

## Already have a project?

Open a file inside your existing CMake, Python, Cargo, SwiftPM, or configured Lua project and use `:RivetTask`. You do not need to create a Rivet manifest to use its native tasks. Follow [Existing projects](/docs/existing-projects/) for discovery and target selection. [How Rivet works with your project](/docs/project-model/) explains file ownership and native tools.

## Update the plugin

For `vim.pack`, run `:lua vim.pack.update({ 'rivet.nvim' })`, review the changes, and confirm with `:write`. For lazy.nvim, use `:Lazy update rivet.nvim`. Restart Neovim afterward. See the [vim.pack update workflow](https://neovim.io/doc/user/pack/#vim.pack-examples) and [lazy.nvim commands](https://lazy.folke.io/usage).

After updating Rivet, review `:RivetUpgrade` separately in each generated project. That command updates supplementary templates while preserving local edits; it does not update the plugin itself. See [project upgrades](/docs/upgrading/) and the [changelog](https://github.com/guzman109/Rivet/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md).

## Local development

To work on the plugin itself, clone the repository and add the checkout to the runtime path:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/guzman109/Rivet.git rivet.nvim
```

```lua
vim.opt.runtimepath:prepend('/absolute/path/to/rivet.nvim')
require('rivet').setup()
```

For the optional terminal interface, follow [Use the CLI](/docs/getting-started/). It needs a separate Lua 5.1+ or LuaJIT interpreter; the Neovim plugin uses Neovim’s bundled Lua runtime.
